\begin{abstract}
Large-scale Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) systems continuously generate sensitive healthcare data that must be securely shared across multiple administrative domains. Existing searchable encryption schemes largely assume centralized search infrastructures and static authorization models, resulting in inefficient encrypted retrieval, costly synchronization, and limited scalability under dynamic IoMT workloads. Moreover, they rarely integrate multi-authority authorization, verifiable retrieval, and adaptive search scheduling within a unified framework. This paper proposes \textbf{MA-LB-PQ-VDSE} (\emph{Multi-Authority Load-Balanced Post-Quantum Verifiable Dynamic Searchable Encryption}), a secure and scalable framework for dynamic IoMT data sharing. The proposed framework unifies multi-authority authorization, policy-bound dynamic searchable encryption, adaptive authorization-aware search scheduling, incremental authorization synchronization, blockchain-backed verifiable retrieval, and post-quantum secure key establishment. By tightly coupling authorization management, encrypted search, dynamic updates, and verification, MA-LB-PQ-VDSE significantly reduces unnecessary index traversal, synchronization overhead, and encrypted-search latency while preserving authorization consistency and retrieval integrity. Furthermore, an adaptive scheduler predicts cryptographic search costs to efficiently distribute encrypted queries across Fog Search Nodes, improving scalability under bursty workloads. Experimental results show that the proposed framework consistently outperforms existing schemes in search latency, throughput, synchronization efficiency, and scalability, demonstrating its practicality for large-scale cross-domain IoMT data sharing.
\end{abstract}


